###  https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_b21a56ed_f969_4612_a6ec_cb7766f7f31d
ns1:EMMO_b21a56ed_f969_4612_a6ec_cb7766f7f31d rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                              rdfs:subClassOf ns1:EMMO_b803f122_4acb_4064_9d71_c1e5fd091fc9 ,
                                                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                owl:onProperty ns1:EMMO_4832e353_6a2d_4deb_9a5b_96989afaff2d ;
                                                                owl:someValuesFrom ns1:EMMO_0527413c_b286_4e9c_b2d0_03fb2a038dee
                                                              ] ,
                                                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                owl:onProperty ns1:EMMO_b8d05d29_9eea_47db_a20b_0b83ceb6de93 ;
                                                                owl:someValuesFrom ns1:EMMO_054af807_85cd_4a13_8eba_119dfdaaf38b
                                                              ] ,
                                                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf ns1:EMMO_9e3ad976_b179_4315_b51e_7e874df583dc
                                                                               ] ;
                                                                owl:someValuesFrom ns1:EMMO_008fd3b2_4013_451f_8827_52bceab11841
                                                              ] ;
                                              rdfs:comment "An 'Physical' that is used as sign (\"semeion\" in greek) that stands for another 'Physical' through an semiotic process."@en ;
                                              rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/reference/electrochemistry-reference#> ;
                                              rdfs:label "Sign"@en ;
                                              skos:example """A novel is made of chapters, paragraphs, sentences, words and characters (in a direct parthood mereological hierarchy).

Each of them are 'sign'-s.

A character can be the a-tomistic 'sign' for the class of texts.

The horizontal segment in the character \"A\" is direct part of \"A\" but it is not a 'sign' itself.

For plain text we can propose the ASCII symbols, for math the fundamental math symbols."""@en ;
                                              skos:prefLabel "Sign"@en ;
                                              ns1:EMMO_8a137e9f_579c_4e28_baca_e8980eb0c3db "EquivalentTo: Index or Conventional or Icon" ;
                                              ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 "An 'Physical' that is used as sign (\"semeion\" in greek) that stands for another 'Physical' through an semiotic process."@en ;
                                              ns1:EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f """A 'Sign' can have temporal-direct-parts which are 'Sign' themselves.

A 'Sign' usually havs 'sign' spatial direct parts only up to a certain elementary semiotic level, in which the part is only a 'Physical' and no more a 'Sign' (i.e. it stands for nothing). This elementary semiotic level is peculiar to each particular system of signs (e.g. text, painting).

Just like an 'Elementary' in the 'Physical' branch, each 'Sign' branch should have an a-tomistic mereological part."""@en ,
                                                                                            """According to Peirce, 'Sign' includes three subcategories:
- symbols: that stand for an object through convention
- indeces: that stand for an object due to causal continguity
- icons: that stand for an object due to similitudes e.g. in shape or composition"""@en .


